# The classic hello world: the program's final value is a greeting.
#
# A script echoes its FINAL value, so this shows "Hello, world!".
# `disp(v)` RETURNS a display form of `v` (for a string, the text
# as-is; for an array, a box diagram of its rank / shape / structure) --
# it does not itself write to stdout, so the greeting shows here only
# because it is the script's last value. For output from a NON-final
# line, use `print(v)`, which writes to stdout directly.
#
# Runs both interpreted and compiled to a native binary -- `disp` and
# strings are supported on the compile path, so both show the same
# text:
#
#   examples/scripts/run.sh examples/hello-world.mlpl            # interpret
#   examples/scripts/run.sh --native examples/hello-world.mlpl  # compile + run

disp("Hello, world!")
